From 331681f8caf42e7d17138f1d3dccfed2f80160b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pooya Parsa <pooya@pi0.io>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:24:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: kwemu ABI 10: the A64 decoded-op tier

kwemu now runs arm64 user code from decoded blocks of up to 64
instructions. The kernel's side of the contract stays: code it rewrites
in place is seen after a flush of a page that maps it. kw_run polls
*irq_pending at every block end (at least every 64 instructions), not
after branches the decoder follows inside a page, and IC IVAU on one
CPU reaches the others through kwemu's own code epoch.
---
 arch/wasm/include/asm/kw_emu.h | 43 ++++++++++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/wasm/include/asm/kw_emu.h b/arch/wasm/include/asm/kw_emu.h
index 8f7b0ebb667..dba5424701c 100644
--- a/arch/wasm/include/asm/kw_emu.h
+++ b/arch/wasm/include/asm/kw_emu.h
@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@
  * guest registers in a kernel-owned struct kw_regs (normally inside the
  * task's pt_regs).
  *
- * Guest ISA (ABI 9): AArch64, A64 instruction set, ARMv8.0-A user mode
+ * Guest ISA (ABI 9 and later): AArch64, A64 instruction set, ARMv8.0-A user mode
  * (EL0), little-endian, with FP and AdvSIMD. No LSE, SVE, pointer
  * authentication or MTE; the PAC and BTI encodings in the hint space run as
  * NOPs. Optional features (KW_ISA_*, below) are enabled only when this
@@ -30,19 +30,34 @@
  * The emulator never writes PTEs: an access with A=0, or a store with
  * D=0, raises KW_EV_FAULT so the kernel can set the bits.
  *
- * Code coherence: the A64 interpreter decodes every instruction from
- * memory as it runs it, so code rewritten by the guest or the kernel is
- * seen at once. Code the kernel rewrites must still be followed by the
- * flushes it does anyway (kw_flush_tlb_range() of a page that maps it,
- * kw_flush_tlb_all(), kw_set_pgd()), and guests make new code visible with
- * IC IVAU (+ ISB), as on hardware with CTR_EL0.DIC = IDC = 0: a later
- * decoded-code cache relies on exactly these points.
+ * Decoded-code cache (ABI 10): kw_run decodes guest code into blocks of up
+ * to 64 instructions inside one page (src/a64/tier.zig) and keeps them in
+ * struct kw_cpu, keyed by virtual pc and trusted only under the exec-TLB
+ * fill they were checked against. Every flush below (kw_flush_tlb_range()
+ * of a page that maps the code, kw_flush_tlb_all(), kw_set_pgd()) makes
+ * each block be checked again (same physical page, same instruction
+ * words) before it runs; kw_set_isa() and kw_host_reset() drop them all.
+ * So:
+ *  - Stores by this CPU's guest code into decoded code are tracked exactly
+ *    and take effect at the next instruction (self-modifying code needs no
+ *    flush), including stores through another virtual alias of the page.
+ *  - Code the kernel writes (or another CPU's guest writes) is seen after
+ *    a flush of a page that maps it on this CPU, or after IC IVAU of the
+ *    line by any guest on any CPU (+ ISB), as on hardware with
+ *    CTR_EL0.DIC = IDC = 0. A kernel that rewrites code in place must
+ *    therefore flush the page (it already must when it changes a mapping).
+ *  - Several CPUs: IC IVAU on one CPU is seen by every other CPU at its
+ *    next decoded-block boundary (at most 64 instructions later) or kw_run
+ *    entry, through a code epoch in kwemu's static data; no kernel action
+ *    is needed.
+ * Decoded blocks hold no state the kernel must save: a task's registers
+ * are in struct kw_regs whenever kw_run returns.
  *
  * Several CPUs (ABI 7): an SMP kernel gives each CPU its own struct
  * kw_cpu, used only by that CPU's thread, on one shared memory. kwemu
  * built with wasm atomics (zig build -Dkwemu-atomics) makes barriers (DMB,
- * DSB) full barriers and is meant to make exclusives atomic; without
- * atomics everything assumes one CPU.
+ * DSB) full barriers, exclusives atomic, and IC IVAU visible to every CPU
+ * (above); without atomics everything assumes one CPU.
  *
  * jit_busy / flush_all (ABI 8): the window in which a CPU compiles JIT code
  * and another CPU may skip waiting for its TLB flush. The A64 build has no
@@ -61,7 +76,7 @@
 extern "C" {
 #endif
 
-#define KW_ABI_VERSION 9
+#define KW_ABI_VERSION 10
 
 /*
  * A64 register state of one task. Fixed layout (offsets in bytes): x[0..30]
@@ -149,8 +164,10 @@ void kw_cpu_init(struct kw_cpu *cpu);
  * cpu->budget is decremented per retired instruction (SVC retires;
  * faulting/illegal instructions do not) and cpu->icount advanced by the
  * same amount; KW_EV_BUDGET when it reaches exactly 0 (returns at once if
- * 0 on entry). *irq_pending is checked on entry, after every taken branch
- * and at least every 64 instructions. Every event leaves regs (incl. pc)
+ * 0 on entry). *irq_pending is checked on entry and at the end of every
+ * decoded block, which is at least every 64 instructions and at every
+ * taken branch out of a block (ABI 10; a branch the decoder followed
+ * inside a page is not a check point). Every event leaves regs (incl. pc)
  * exactly at an instruction boundary. Data accesses may be unaligned
  * (SCTLR_EL1.A = 0); SP alignment is not checked.
  */
-- 
